"""Builds pilots/L07/job.json — The Disappearance of Glenn Miller (long-form, ~7-8 min).
Sources: Skeptoid #406; Everything Everywhere 'The Disappearance of Glenn Miller' (incl. Dennis Spragg 2017 study);
Tara Ross TDIH; This Day in Aviation 15 Dec 1944."""
import json, pathlib, re
CH = [
 ("Into the clouds", """[serious] On the afternoon of December 15th, 1944, a small single-engine plane took off from an airfield in the English countryside. It was cold and grey. Fog had grounded flights for days. On board were three men. A pilot. An Army colonel. And the most famous bandleader in the world. Major Glenn Miller was on his way to Paris, to get ready for a Christmas concert for the troops. The plane flew south, into the cloud, and out over the English Channel. It never arrived. No wreckage was found. No bodies. No distress call. For more than eighty years, people have argued about what happened to Glenn Miller. A storm. A broken engine. A pilot lost in the fog. Bombs falling from the sky. Even a secret death in Paris, covered up by the Army. This is what the records actually show, and what they don't."""),
 ("The sound of a generation", """To understand why this disappearance shook the world, you have to understand how big Glenn Miller was. He was born in Iowa in 1904, a trombone player who spent years arranging music for other people's bands. In New York he studied with the composer Joseph Schillinger, and one of the pieces he wrote in that period, Miller's Tune, would later be rearranged and renamed. Moonlight Serenade. His own first band, in 1937, failed. So he went back to the drawing board and invented a sound. A clarinet on top, saxophones underneath. Smooth, warm, instantly recognizable. It was called the Miller Sound, and it took over America. In 1940, his record Tuxedo Junction sold a hundred and fifteen thousand copies in its first week. In 1942, Chattanooga Choo Choo became the first record ever to earn a gold disc, after selling more than a million copies. Time magazine reported that two to six of the records in America's jukeboxes were usually his. Moonlight Serenade. In the Mood. These weren't just songs. They were the sound of a generation. He made films in Hollywood, Sun Valley Serenade and Orchestra Wives. He was earning around twenty thousand dollars a week. And then, at the peak of all of it, he gave it up."""),
 ("Captain Miller", """In 1942, after America entered the war, Miller wanted to serve. He was thirty-eight years old, married, with children, and his eyesight was poor. The Navy turned him down. So he wrote to the Army. In September 1942, he was commissioned as a captain. He wanted to modernize military music, to bring the swing that young soldiers loved to the men who were fighting. He built the Army Air Force band, and in 1944 it was sent to Britain. They played concerts for the troops, and broadcast on the radio, including programs beamed at German soldiers. General Eisenhower himself had asked for the band to be sent. By late 1944, the Allies had liberated Paris, and Miller wanted to move the band there, closer to the troops. The plan was approved. The band recorded around eighty hours of music in advance, so the broadcasts could continue while they moved. By the twelfth of December, the work was done. All that was left was for Miller to fly ahead to Paris and prepare. But the weather would not cooperate. Flights on the thirteenth and fourteenth of December were cancelled."""),
 ("The last flight", """On the fifteenth, Lieutenant Colonel Norman Baessell had a flight booked to France on a small plane, a single-engine UC-64 Norseman. He offered Miller a seat. The pilot was Flight Officer John Morgan. The weather was still poor. Low cloud and drizzle. A Norseman was a rugged little utility plane, but it was not built for flying blind through bad weather. They took off from Twinwood Farm, an airfield near Bedford, just before two in the afternoon. An observer on the coast later reported a small plane heading out over the Channel from Beachy Head. Decades later, the diary of a teenage plane spotter near Reading turned up, recording a Norseman passing that day. And that is the last anyone knows. The plane never reached Paris. And here is the strangest part. Nobody noticed. The next morning, Germany launched a massive surprise attack in the Ardennes. The Battle of the Bulge. Everyone's attention was elsewhere. Miller was not realized to be missing until the eighteenth of December. His wife was told on the twenty-third. The public found out on Christmas Eve."""),
 ("The theories", """With no wreck and no bodies, the theories started almost at once. The most dramatic came decades later. A British navigator, Fred Shaw, said that on the same day, his Lancaster bomber was returning from a cancelled raid, and the bombers dumped their bombs over the Channel. He said he saw a small plane flying below, and watched it go down. It's a powerful story. But there are problems. It rests on the memory of one man. There's no record of him telling it until years after the war. Other airmen who flew that day said the visibility was so poor they could barely see their own wings. And researchers have found the timing difficult to match. Then there are the darker theories. That Miller made it to Paris, and died there in embarrassing circumstances, and the Army covered it up. That he was on a secret mission to Germany. There is no real evidence for any of these. The 1997 German newspaper story behind one of them was later disowned by the journalist whose name was on it, who said he had been misquoted. Theories like these say more about how much people loved Glenn Miller than about what happened to him. It was hard to accept that a man that famous could simply vanish, with no explanation at all."""),
 ("What the file tells us", """So what most likely happened? In 2017, researcher Dennis Spragg of the Glenn Miller Archive published a detailed study. He found the route the plane took was unusual, and that the pilot was not certified to fly by instruments alone, in cloud, with no view of the ground. The Norseman also had a known weakness. In cold, wet conditions, ice could form in its carburetor and starve the engine. Over the freezing Channel, in thick cloud, a pilot could lose his sense of up and down, or an engine could quietly die. Either way, a small plane would hit the water in moments. There would be no time for a radio call. And the Channel would keep everything. It's not a satisfying answer. Legends rarely end with bad weather and a tired engine. But it's the answer that fits the evidence. Glenn Miller is still officially listed as missing in action. He was awarded the Bronze Star after his death. Memorials to him stand at the American cemetery in Cambridge, England, and at Arlington. In 1954, Hollywood told his story in The Glenn Miller Story, with James Stewart in the lead. His Army band kept playing until 1946, and that year his estate authorized a new Glenn Miller Orchestra to carry on his music. And every Christmas, somewhere, In the Mood still plays. So what do you think happened over the Channel? Tell me in the comments. This is Dossier Decoded."""),
]
